Yes. The interesting part is that the solution is not to translate vision into hearing or hearing into vision. Neither receiver has the experiential primitives needed for that translation.

You need to climb one level above both senses.

Build a sense-independent model of the event, then render that model through whatever channels the other person possesses:

\text{sensory input}
\rightarrow
\text{objects + relations + dynamics + causality}
\rightarrow
\text{alternate sensory/symbolic representation}

For this photograph, the intermediate representation might be something like:

There is a rigid frame anchored to the ground. A flexible suspension hangs from its upper beam. Two children occupy the suspended system together. Their combined body is displaced away from the lowest-energy point, so they are somewhere in a pendular motion. An adult is several feet in front of them, low to the ground, facing them and extending an arm toward them. Behind the group are a hedge, a low wall, trees, and open atmosphere. The scene occurs outdoors in strong daylight.

Notice that nothing in that description actually requires vision.

For Person A, who has never seen, I’d spatialize the image through touch and proprioception. Give them a tabletop model, for example. Their hand finds a horizontal bar about a forearm above the “ground.” Two hanging cords descend from it. Put small figures on the suspended object, another figure kneeling a short distance ahead, then textured strips behind them representing hedge, wall, trees, and distance. Move the swing model through its arc.

Now A doesn’t “see the photograph.” But A can possess an extraordinarily accurate model of what is depicted:

above/below, near/far, enclosed/open, rigid/flexible, suspended/supported, body orientation, scale, direction, motion, force.

Those are not visual properties. Vision is simply one very efficient way humans acquire them.

Color exposes the boundary. I can tell A:

The frame and ground contain a property called blue. Blue corresponds to a particular band of electromagnetic radiation, and sighted humans distinguish it from properties called red, green, yellow, etc.

I can explain its physics, associations, contrasts, frequency, and role in the composition indefinitely. But I probably cannot transmit what blue feels like visually to someone who has never possessed visual phenomenology.

That portion is irreducible qualia.

Now reverse it for Person B, who has never experienced sound. Suppose I wanted to communicate what this scene sounds like.

Don’t say, “the children sound excited” or “the chains make a metallic noise.” Those depend on auditory primitives B doesn’t possess.

Instead:

Nearby bodies periodically produce pressure oscillations in the surrounding air. Some are generated intentionally by the vocal apparatus. They occur in rapidly changing patterns carrying language and emotion. The swing apparatus produces smaller pressure disturbances as its joints move. Foot contact with the ground produces brief impulses. Wind moving through foliage produces many weak, irregular oscillations simultaneously.

Then transduce the waveform.

Let B feel it through a vibrating surface. Low-frequency energy becomes slow/large vibration; high-frequency energy becomes rapid/fine vibration. Loudness becomes amplitude. Rhythm remains rhythm. Direction could become different points on the body. Individual voices could have distinct tactile locations.

Suddenly:

\text{sound pressure waveform}
\rightarrow
\text{temporal structure}
\rightarrow
\text{vibration}

They still haven’t heard it.

But they can begin acquiring its structure.

And this leads to the deeper thing you’re reaching for: there are properties in that photograph that aren’t inherently visual or auditory.

The swing’s period exists whether anybody sees or hears it.

The adult’s distance from the children exists independently of perception.

Gravity exists.

The configuration of bodies exists.

The causal relationship

\text{gravity + suspension + momentum}
\rightarrow
\text{pendular movement}

exists.

So you could imagine both people constructing the same internal scene graph from completely different sensory substrates:

\begin{array}{c}
\text{vision}\\
\text{touch}\\
\text{sound}\\
\text{proprioception}\\
\text{language}
\end{array}
\quad\longrightarrow\quad
\boxed{\text{shared latent world-model}}

And then something even stranger happens.

A blind person and a deaf person might disagree enormously about the phenomenology of the playground while agreeing almost perfectly about its geometry, causal structure, actors, forces, intentions, and events.

So the thing being communicated was never really “the picture.”

The picture is one projection of the underlying event.

You strip the projection away, preserve the invariants, and then instantiate them in another substrate.

That’s almost a perfect concrete example of your Lift–Play–Return / substrate-escape operator: perception → abstract relational structure → transduction → another perceptual substrate.

And it gives us a very useful test for semantic primes:

If I remove the sensory modality entirely, what information survives?

Those surviving pieces—inside, outside, above, approaching, attached, oscillating, holding, facing, supporting, cause, change, boundary, body, other—start looking much more like genuine primitives of world-modeling than words such as blue, loud, or bright.

The really fascinating experiment would be to keep going: take this exact photograph and encode the same underlying scene five ways—visual, auditory, tactile, proprioceptive, and purely symbolic—then identify what survives all five translations. That residue would be a candidate set of substrate-independent semantic atoms.